#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6cd156-8ca4-5855-a3cc-5f0d395f4aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324341} {:answer/id #uuid "317a4675-7255-5700-9ad9-8cfade1093ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324370} {:answer/id #uuid "68507ca4-abf8-5cd6-8193-9965c1c024dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324842} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34229-1bb6-551f-bdc6-3c2c3f4134b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeccb5b-f4c0-5768-a9a9-bdc8a37556ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d160d2-e817-535b-93be-44d3599c92c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c31eda49-a231-50f2-81e8-83c448690ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39673" :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e5bd11-b4a5-5c3f-97c8-baf947fdb648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324361} {:answer/id #uuid "ce09c054-3fce-534d-ab95-741a3083a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325760} {:answer/id #uuid "dc484259-e0ad-507c-9124-07e7bfbf313a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325892} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5488aa-cd87-5a5b-9265-c03f48ae0f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9c2e80-2a3e-55ba-bc7f-3b7949f67a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f3e9a2-5330-5b08-8e51-b27a94666da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12ff35df-51f6-5e85-9109-59feb724214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39679" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t\\cdot {e}^{{t}^{3}\\cdot t}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{4}}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{e}^{{t}^{4}}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[3{e}^{(1)^{4}}+(1)]-[3{e}^{(0)^{4}}+(0)] \\\\  & =[3e+1]-[3] \\\\  & =3e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e03bb2-38fd-590c-a711-0f08ab8bba0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330278} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfe119c-4c83-5685-9e72-3d7e3a36aafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331355} {:answer/id #uuid "b81ff5e8-6e1a-5415-a73c-95423efd63bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331574} {:answer/id #uuid "d88e6b38-60e3-55a1-b267-396d75df9e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331872} {:answer/id #uuid "e8104679-8097-5d99-8871-6a598728a76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331872} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2900df1-3dd3-5e4a-bcd5-28bec34e44ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "105b4be6-43d2-5e3c-baa6-177cc086c196" :question/math-academy-id "q-49660" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-x} & ⇒ & v=-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})-\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-x{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0}+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0}-{e}^{-x}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =[-0{e}^{-0}-2{e}^{2}]-[{e}^{0}-{e}^{2}] \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}-1+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d04d3d4-1dd0-57fb-b1e2-c225a604887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330065} {:answer/id #uuid "10711776-d5af-58ee-a204-f0481a37ef0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330095} {:answer/id #uuid "94d2489f-97d4-5923-a4e5-c84c3ac1206a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331268} {:answer/id #uuid "bef58735-ea92-5021-ba86-331e3acbbeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331643} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ab2f9-1ace-5545-9427-562efa040d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331643} :answer-field/id #uuid "05dc3940-684e-5059-b3ec-b817a2dd3311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b8bc1c4-8b9c-5e47-93e0-c4965c12347b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49669" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into  the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+{\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[0-0]+\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac416bd-1ef2-598d-9769-0fc6af1898a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330183} {:answer/id #uuid "92f021d2-f657-568a-9458-97dc25ae2244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331255} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f49c5b-b91f-5372-945d-fd51d107045f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331524} {:answer/id #uuid "bc706a10-6660-59a2-b283-dfcba28564b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331611} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ff4fa6-edc2-533d-88c9-09406c898508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331524} :answer-field/id #uuid "e116d325-1e59-5f3b-b1ef-e63b4cf776b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b7d1b926-6342-572b-ba02-f2edcfbab3a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49674" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]+\\frac{1}{4}[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[0]+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e6d4ed-2a95-5b96-a81c-b22982fa0499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330291} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20740e-c6da-5ad1-a62b-85285c7d638d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330660} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6c93f5-fe12-5a2e-9367-75108b2be7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331383} {:answer/id #uuid "e330c811-9644-5c56-b0f4-0f24b313cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331956} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9e871c-2c0e-518b-a4af-68c0103a8640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331956} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a6308-1549-5a6a-ab50-2e8d6d61c150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4518fd0-a820-5329-a38a-c80d052e166a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49676" :question/problem "Evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)] \\\\  & +[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]+[1-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ecbfbfa-cec6-5df3-a0c9-e11c1f67b71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330075} {:answer/id #uuid "4f85b6ee-800b-57ab-a844-ec6e2709c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330672} {:answer/id #uuid "80f169d6-295c-563d-8914-8d2bd9999054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331093} {:answer/id #uuid "ba59d7eb-18ba-53f2-95b6-75daba77e7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331594} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1d4456-3d28-5aa2-8934-92baec9cfff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331594} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4db82c-4c9f-5857-a947-4a090d21993d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "622f5ee2-fdb3-59b2-9462-4de78b4bcda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49677" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1/2)}-0\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{2(1/2)}-{e}^{2\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{e}{2}-0]-\\frac{1}{4}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}-\\frac{e-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-e+1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2314e2c3-071d-5d1e-bdca-e7896981bcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330252} {:answer/id #uuid "79f50141-9381-5317-8323-d88e90e3a8a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331031} {:answer/id #uuid "8b21378b-3425-5e03-a842-b62254df4ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331184} {:answer/id #uuid "b85d4fc1-2e09-58f6-acfa-bed34a4d5787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331579} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d6949-0735-57ba-84b8-0d9c36850ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331676} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe032e0-af40-56ec-a6ec-e09e79cb742f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b98f2873-3564-5f19-a00b-95639d25a774" :question/math-academy-id "q-49682" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & +2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]+2[0-0] \\\\  & =2(-2\\pi )+0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce3b46d-4467-59a9-895b-8e79ecd42cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333980} {:answer/id #uuid "79d35268-9837-5695-b2e1-ba4fd135db1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334084} {:answer/id #uuid "7d41aec1-2a1f-5c42-b1e3-bea7fecb46bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334107} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a4bfed-bffb-5ed0-bd6f-96c238f743b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334886} {:answer/id #uuid "e041aab9-97de-5648-aa5a-aa8400305391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334084} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85409c4-a65c-5eb7-9b82-9b48b4641df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "858abb55-da23-5f83-a5ad-a58ea846deb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-64907" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,0,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot 1-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz) \\\\  & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot (z) \\\\  & =yz-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz) \\\\  & =z(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,0,\\pi )} & =\\pi (0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi (0-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ef7585-f490-52ce-84d4-c14afbd71f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333143} {:answer/id #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea7c594-0a56-5e0b-a1fd-7fd5413db99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333580} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a4ec80-da82-5689-8ecb-e79e98294b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335176} {:answer/id #uuid "fe69f0a9-435b-5c17-9a63-9c54c8b0d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f1d221-bfab-57b4-bd66-7eea20107505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "838d032d-5600-5cb9-9af4-36cc70864ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65131" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f09aafd-c1e5-5dd1-bf03-b73681599bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451477} {:answer/id #uuid "2f54d447-a39c-55bb-850f-c236243803eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451477} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c75200-01ed-54ca-9326-1eca1bb956f5" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf599bcb-83ed-5f4a-a06d-a9db97c5fe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451479} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ec1810-7d26-56cd-aaac-4334475ae5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451480} :answer-field/id #uuid "a87ea30f-e244-5f86-bffc-4a5395ff286d" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca681fe9-ee18-536a-9299-af74510e99ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451481} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5393a0-13ec-5e60-9584-47126e03236e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451481} :answer-field/id #uuid "118478a6-d6eb-5b08-b5c7-fcce661c9509" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ccd0f4b9-0dbd-51e2-a95c-14471c65ba52" :question/math-academy-id "q-65145" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3784325a-a369-50b5-91bd-19b0506f0065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f25158-cf49-5e7b-a678-2a7edff63f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334412} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b97395-2591-5fe7-a765-24e75cd37f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334425} {:answer/id #uuid "e42e7344-8685-5612-9437-27f80a5f22bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334977} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b5911-a43a-5716-a002-b2a98cb3a797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d7321b-1e15-5d1e-9dec-8cc8fec04036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f338c4bb-049a-5229-858d-81a11a4613ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-65168" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a75fd68-5ec7-5632-b2b1-fef51b87521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333535} {:answer/id #uuid "5170c32f-b36f-5ca4-a08a-fa1a93c89b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333735} {:answer/id #uuid "961de4aa-f74a-598e-bd2e-c4eb3429141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334326} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b33c3f-c32b-5340-88f8-af70fdc09ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334613} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a0a9fc-fe21-5d8a-acf3-90f18615df49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334326} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b9d346-84ac-5de6-aa10-5343d535f534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "663cf0be-bb05-570b-8d9c-c9b9ecc4ae37" :question/math-academy-id "q-6889" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecda314aeaaa79332a51386926218175bb0ffd6ce0e564b069a643c53f23b642.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447870c5-0e16-591a-8300-97b1a1b53090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333622} {:answer/id #uuid "548b03ca-4238-5b00-9cb4-91def2cc07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14\\sqrt{2}$ and $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333761} {:answer/id #uuid "68381630-1c58-5c47-b731-887a1f1decb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333945} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa132ec-2b5e-506f-9009-d06c05f62c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333966} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f55ce-3720-511d-baaa-529e5639ba46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7\\sqrt{2}$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333622} :answer-field/id #uuid "c27addb0-e02b-555b-8abb-615b89631ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50aac069-cc6f-51ae-b729-84e72b0989dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-69563" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89dca9db-88dc-5f58-9f23-ec98ca1bbc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438478} {:answer/id #uuid "128f9548-3706-5b6d-94e4-afd0ea31e354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438479} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8abcdf3-f784-54de-90bb-b51f314f8912" :answer-field/key "$M$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439268} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b74bddc-ad74-5f61-a0ce-c1bb3f545d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438480} {:answer/id #uuid "f99619dc-7a17-50df-b37c-d5f5007df859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438480} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b81975-4ed5-5bfd-b978-ee8fdac7cf77" :answer-field/key "$det(M)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad1a88c7-0f27-56c5-b163-10cc23842fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438482} {:answer/id #uuid "97a592ec-ef1e-56af-8795-1ae139a9168a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438483} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e3b518-970c-5ddb-8ddd-c289c427c026" :answer-field/key "$det(M)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "924829bc-4eac-54c3-82c8-f773955ca97e" :question/math-academy-id "q-69834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$M$ is a singular matrix” and $det(M)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20394717-12ca-5041-b23f-416aa83995b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333278} {:answer/id #uuid "45f72ead-a726-5b03-9e54-669f062a7bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333636} {:answer/id #uuid "5efc1e85-e466-5527-bfee-b216fc63bee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333854} {:answer/id #uuid "6f183c3b-27ac-5c2a-91b0-f1eac914fd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334001} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2e1340-0645-5d50-95ed-65fc11a6a4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333854} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a765e3d-d8c0-512a-9934-b557f6c6eeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2872275-2ccf-570a-873b-56e8f9665a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-69891" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9eb144bd4109846f3e1af4fb7d8ed70d2d8a29c3804547b89238d6437c4258d8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b3bb62-33b0-5fec-8deb-ae6cde1bb204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333142} {:answer/id #uuid "57b12094-7cc6-57f1-9a04-7d107daa9163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333789} {:answer/id #uuid "723d2d46-f03f-5f70-9a86-d1649d7ce1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334024} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a9b15c-1941-5024-81d9-ee6347a6b3e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334669} {:answer/id #uuid "ff431699-49d9-55e7-9957-c34cb6f5df16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335235} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa364a8-de07-5a0a-aec8-fc0fb7bebdf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "daa08063-18f2-5c3f-bfa5-03e9e47d2153" :question/math-academy-id "q-69892" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1acc2d6b8387947ff7186322796e273bcec366810ad73ac0025494ab627e06b2.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01216287-0f50-583c-9086-c9c0e4cc5138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332999} {:answer/id #uuid "308f5905-a1ef-5cd4-bcbf-93f14e467471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333438} {:answer/id #uuid "853926c0-f101-5e2d-baf4-3a46b814eb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334174} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca9f7c3-1c08-5f19-869f-2b8bb488f6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334242} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a37e6e-54ed-5aa9-98f6-bcb6e68f2dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332999} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c562ed-e50c-5f34-9608-c043330e7886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc59b788-b5c5-5dc7-bac3-9ab39eac60d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-69893" :question/problem "![](images/be/be8468d84e3820bd1e9181d31aa3a1b7d1a85c743020a69cb67fc3f601a88363.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108d7ae9-a232-5e60-a939-66c340b257d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336190} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5eccf-eb1f-5b09-992c-5886e6477e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336746} {:answer/id #uuid "990c829b-454f-5fa4-985e-5a8b68b776dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337424} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98312c-c9f2-57e2-af6f-1c2737cbfe55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{261}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337475} {:answer/id #uuid "e64d8662-a67b-5f89-82e8-43f6670bbf96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{97}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336746} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc2c532-03ad-50fc-87d1-ea179d58d664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "874ee269-4d1d-55a1-8553-04a0864d5dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71106" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,WXY$, if $W(2,-3,1)$, $X(-2,1,2)$, $Y(3,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =x-w \\\\  & =(-2i+j+2k)-(2i-3j+k) \\\\  & =-4i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(3i-j+3k)-(2i-3j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2)i-((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =6i+9j-12k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0932fe92-f8de-576e-baff-260c08cc1de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336112} {:answer/id #uuid "7efd8fb5-fe32-562c-9d4b-d5fba1fd6b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337184} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef6d298-4c28-5ba4-8962-81bdffb4b6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337321} {:answer/id #uuid "eb23c031-3731-5c6e-aed8-4d36c5b9f20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338081} {:answer/id #uuid "fc78233b-b5f1-5862-b741-cfd6e52f48e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0daa5bc7-b875-5ff1-9f3e-508ca3073b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d10b6fa-62e6-5893-8115-0db23250352f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71107" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,NPQ$, if $N(1,0,1)$, $P(2,2,3)$, $Q(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP} & =p-n \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =q-n \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)i-(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,NPQ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aed134b-e703-50a2-978e-28071145ddef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336586} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfc4a3f-63ff-5d1a-9c0f-ebcdda99431e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336748} {:answer/id #uuid "7047a495-50ca-567e-88e4-273aa8914f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337028} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ffce1-6601-5f91-8b10-271b848d8b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337703} {:answer/id #uuid "ca325988-d63a-57fe-a35a-615af188b50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336748} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c086ce-f13d-57ca-a69b-b4e1945642fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d25fe3fb-23c6-5ca6-a0b3-475c85a18297" :question/math-academy-id "q-71109" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,FGH$, if $F(1,1,2)$, $G(2,2,3)$, $H(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =g-f \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =h-f \\\\  & =(i+k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)j+(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,FGH$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e924de-54b8-5328-8007-3b54077fff81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336697} {:answer/id #uuid "55f44d06-e6f6-5fe2-85b3-f7a48edceca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336822} {:answer/id #uuid "d8658307-2241-5976-8f8b-323e902dd088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc896c2a-9e50-5f5d-a2dd-8fde07578b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338251} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6bcc4-3401-51e2-8ab5-cc86c889fcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336697} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4de416-4232-5a36-bef2-7154753e04a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c8d8ac3-0a22-56d4-bfd2-5a54455f0999" :question/math-academy-id "q-71111" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,CDE$, if $C(1,-2,3)$, $D(1,-3,3)$, $E(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-3j+3k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =e-c \\\\  & =(i+j+k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bce9a27-f4cc-58b8-899b-0adcf77a3029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336444} {:answer/id #uuid "3154ea6f-18ff-5b87-b29e-4d415f805d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336503} {:answer/id #uuid "8127319e-f64a-5b03-9442-11dd8802bec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337204} {:answer/id #uuid "9c703731-367d-5c95-8dba-8382da5e1070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337446} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccd42e4-4e86-5ccb-92e1-e81125b47e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337446} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6a4426-4d5c-5b55-8aaf-f5663e17d82d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2109dfb7-8989-5063-a0ad-becd345c3aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71113" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,3,2)$, $M(1,1,2)$, $N(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d3204-08b4-5aac-aca7-bdc773125774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336085} {:answer/id #uuid "62d5a2fd-d0ed-50a1-b9e1-530565c72f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336920} {:answer/id #uuid "8101cc36-fb71-50d7-ac71-47da39f98581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337201} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d8bec1-6d0d-54d7-ae45-5d08e1b8540e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337627} {:answer/id #uuid "e626fb9e-e952-533a-955b-4d34b400fc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336085} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2232b9a-6e70-56f9-95d7-f89ee74428fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "86adad36-11f0-5e43-80fd-01317a16e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71115" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VH$ of the $△\\,TUV$, if $T(3,2,3)$, $U(0,1,0)$, $V(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =j-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-3i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(2i+j+2k)-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093467b9-0067-542c-b65c-633c1cfc4b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336113} {:answer/id #uuid "3859e15a-56e5-569e-825a-f822ed3eac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336557} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2eb1b1-c7b8-5381-b418-d9244be8d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336991} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d294a1-32a0-5b4f-94b7-ee30ffc9011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} {:answer/id #uuid "de85db2c-4065-5f60-92b7-7fdad6d31158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1b4065-470d-599f-81c3-c9f0fd87b16f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9999542b-9f9f-5d86-95f6-bcd8d4190efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71116" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $ZH$ of the $△\\,WYZ$, if $W(2,3,2)$, $Y(1,2,1)$, $Z(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =z-w \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-4j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d340e7-ddb4-51cf-bbf6-01458c17f611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336084} {:answer/id #uuid "0d900ac9-ddc4-5d0b-8b81-7b341697e575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336159} {:answer/id #uuid "56354dd4-647e-56d7-a9fd-a95bbb4ca8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336825} {:answer/id #uuid "8125d199-48f3-5aad-b0c5-ab92c3d59078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337203} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8b3bae-3fde-5b2f-8f26-3e3448abad77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337203} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6e1490-16c3-533e-862d-6938fcc3b920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f34d7ce2-3b18-5fda-a701-f95afc0575b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71117" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $GH$ of the $△\\,GSM$, if $S(1,-1,2)$, $G(-2,0,2)$, $M(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =g-s \\\\  & =(-2i+2k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+j \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =m-s \\\\  & =(i+j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3c7d45-2508-558a-a671-704a2f709aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336627} {:answer/id #uuid "733fefab-043d-5fe4-93ed-cf5c6a3298cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337070} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0454d2-6cd6-50e2-9a7f-17ac15f3a137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337812} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fb8394-0a4f-5554-bef2-b4565678aeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} {:answer/id #uuid "f7adc41f-f450-52b1-9898-009b7b23875a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e3142c-b7af-51c6-a353-db8d28d28403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6da0a984-327a-597f-9055-73fa298d90dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71128" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $DH$ of the $△\\,DMA$, if $D(-1,2,1)$, $M(-1,0,-1)$, $A(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =d-m \\\\  & =(-i+2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =a-m \\\\  & =(i-2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157bc3da-2d1c-5137-b516-d8ff7c42bad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336235} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe7ebf-3262-597f-b2fd-edf534fd618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336607} {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} {:answer/id #uuid "829583d7-8e78-579d-9221-64ce8b48dc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337220} {:answer/id #uuid "e26af925-4997-53e5-8b3d-7425b92d8e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48649d2-bf76-55fc-aef9-b0aff8e82c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22cfc584-e8e6-55e0-a1d3-35d71c5742a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71370" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049b7f23-d6a7-5be3-a6ca-ac19b8a84234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336070} {:answer/id #uuid "123c36c6-7310-581c-8de4-b675e090dc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336203} {:answer/id #uuid "4b49d759-d015-5843-b6e9-3497dc09edfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730} {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3f738-ef19-5d09-afcb-04a5b6bdd7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} :answer-field/id #uuid "60184a90-8959-560b-aa4a-378830d13dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "436e814d-020d-5b28-b909-495bbb1c431a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71374" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2372ab35-9f15-566a-9150-808733fd4bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336363} {:answer/id #uuid "2630b03f-d66e-5733-b918-78baaf9f7ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336392} {:answer/id #uuid "e77b993b-c3bb-5f5a-9474-9bd52741e5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338055} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097253-bc27-5ce4-8fb8-daded67d09c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338066} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a289db-1c93-5c45-ab8f-caa51a0ee7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d94b131-873d-56a9-92e8-a2ed25af773a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1092dcda-9470-51be-bb7f-4be55bcc6069" :question/math-academy-id "q-71381" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
